window.EXPLAINER_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"k":"page","t":"v1/worker: the attention worker and runner","u":"code/attention-worker.html","s":"Three files run the attention role: the worker that swaps in the AFD runner, the runner that wraps native Model Runner V2, and the shared metadata mixin that publishes the control plane.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Advanced","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through the main methods","AFDAttentionWorker.init_device: swap the runner class","execute_model: scope the context, then delegate","send_dp_metadata: publish one control payload","Deep dive","The overrides and what each adds","The shared mixin, used by GPU and NPU","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"afd_plugin/config.py: the AFD config schema and parser","u":"code/config-py.html","s":"config.py defines AFDConfig and the functions that parse and validate additional_config[\"afd\"]. config_utils.py holds the type coercers; envs.py holds two diagnostic environment flags. This page reads the three files.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through the main methods","AFDConfig and its aliases","_normalize_mapping","validate_afd_config","config_utils.coerce_extra_int","Deep dive","The parser entry points, and when each is used","Environment flags in envs.py","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"connectors/base.py, factory.py, metadata.py: the connector core","u":"code/connectors-core.html","s":"Three files hold the connector contract, the factory and the shared metadata objects. This page maps the files and reads the metadata objects and the factory loader.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through the main methods","AFDTransferMetadata: the per-transfer record","encode_control_payload: the wire format","AFDConnectorFactory: name to class","Deep dive","The metadata objects at a glance","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"model_executor/models/deepseek_v2.py: the DeepSeek wrapper","u":"code/deepseek-v2-py.html","s":"This file holds the DeepSeek AFD wrapper: the role-aware decoder layer, the remote FFN proxies, the remote-experts MoE shell, and the model and causal-LM classes that filter weights by role.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Advanced","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through the main methods","Construction branches: what each role builds","The layer's compute methods, by role","The model and causal-LM wrappers","get_experts_layer_indices and get_experts_routing_spec","Deep dive","The remote proxies, compared","The weight filter in detail","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"v1/worker: the FFN worker and runner","u":"code/ffn-worker.html","s":"Three files run the FFN role: the worker that starts the daemon loop, the stand-alone connector-driven runner, and the CPU-safe token-count helpers that map attention counts to FFN ranks.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Advanced","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through the main methods","_run_ffn_server_loop: one step per control payload","_ffn_forward: layers x stages","aggregate_ffn_token_counts: attention counts to FFN ranks","Deep dive","Why the worker and runner both reject a no-control-plane connector","The token-count helpers","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The code map: folders, sizes and dependencies","u":"code/index.html","s":"The folder tree of afd-plugin at the pinned commit, with file and line counts, a GPU/NPU/shared split, and how the folders depend on each other.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"","h":["Purpose","The folder tree","How the folders depend on each other","The GPU / NPU / shared split","How the numbers were measured","The module pages","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"afd_plugin/__init__.py: the plugin entry module","u":"code/init-py.html","s":"The package init holds register_afd (the entry point vLLM calls), the AFD model table, and the opt-in spawn override. This page reads it step by step.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through register_afd","Part 1: Guard, import safety and version check","Part 2: Patches, routing, DBO, the NPU patch and the models","The model registration table","The spawn override","Deep dive","Error paths: what is caught, and what is not","Import safety and environment variables","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"connectors/gpu/p2p.py: the GPU P2P connector","u":"code/p2p-py.html","s":"The GPU connector moves hidden states with vLLM's NCCL wrapper. This page maps the class layout, the collective init, the four data calls, the control plane and the custom ops.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Advanced","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through the main methods","init_afd_connector: the collective setup","The four data calls","P2pNcclAFDControlPlane: the DP-metadata path","_register_p2p_custom_ops: four ops, once per process","Deep dive","The process groups and communicators","Error paths","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"afd_plugin/validation.py: runtime wiring checks","u":"code/validation-py.html","s":"validation.py holds the CPU-safe checks for the AFD runtime stack: the worker FQCN constants, the GPU and NPU model-runner validators, the worker-selection mapper, and assert_compatible_afd_stack. This page reads the GPU parts in full and names the NPU parts.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["Purpose","Where it sits","Who calls it","What it calls","Key classes and functions","Walk through the main methods","The FQCN constants","validate_gpu_model_runner_v2_config, rule by rule","assert_compatible_afd_stack","afd_worker_qualname_for_platform_default","The NPU validator (NPU scope)","Deep dive","The GPU rules as an error-path table","BATCH_SIZE_FACTOR and the CAM receive capacity","Related concepts","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"AFD configuration: additional_config[\"afd\"]","u":"concepts/afd-config.html","s":"AFD reads one key, additional_config[\"afd\"]. This page shows its schema, the compatibility aliases, how values are normalized and coerced, the validation rules, and the rule that AFD is active only when the key is present.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: The schema","Step 2: Aliases","Step 3: Normalization and coercion","Step 4: The validation rules","Step 5: AFD is active only when the key is present","Step 6: Per-connector options","Deep dive","Trace one recipe value end to end: num_attention_ranks = 2","async_dp is NPU-only, and false on GPU","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The attention runtime: a seam over Model Runner V2","u":"concepts/attention-runtime.html","s":"The attention runner is a thin subclass of the native vLLM GPU Model Runner V2. It overrides a few seams for the connector and the control plane, and delegates the model forward, sampling and output to the native class.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Construction makes the connector and checks for a control plane","Step 2: load_model defers the connector rendezvous to the end","Step 3: execute_model wraps native V2 in the AFD context","Step 4: The AFD metadata provider installs the sidecar","Step 5: The control plane publishes once per step","Deep dive","_use_afd_execution_context, scope by scope","How DBO and graph replay plug in","What stays native","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Compat patches: how AFD changes vLLM without editing it","u":"concepts/compat-patches.html","s":"AFD edits no vLLM source. It imports patch modules that replace vLLM functions in place, behind a version gate. This page covers the discipline, the gates, and what each GPU patch changes.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Patch discipline","Step 2: Two version checks","Step 3: config_validation","Step 4: engine_core","Step 5: gpu_mrv2_ubatching","Step 6: async_dp patches","Step 7: NPU patches","Deep dive","The install gate and the hasattr guards","The monkey-patch rule: why order matters","The full patch table","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The connector contract: the Attention/FFN transport","u":"concepts/connector-contract.html","s":"One base class sets the contract that every AFD connector follows. This page shows the lifecycle, the four data-path calls, the optional control plane, the config hook and the factory.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: One base class, three jobs","Step 2: The lifecycle","Step 3: The attention-side calls","Step 4: The FFN-side calls","Step 5: The optional control plane","Step 6: The config hook","Step 7: The factory","Deep dive","The full call order, side by side","Worked example: hidden-state shapes for DeepSeek-V2-Lite, 2A2F","The three connectors, compared","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The control plane: DP metadata once per step","u":"concepts/control-plane.html","s":"Before each layer loop, the attention side sends per-stage token counts to the FFN side. This page shows what the control plane carries, both publish paths, the wire format and the receive side.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Once per step, before the layers","Step 2: What the payload carries","Step 3: The two attention publish paths","Step 4: The wire format and the separate group","Step 5: The FFN side receives and derives the shapes","Step 6: The async NPU connector has no control plane","Deep dive","The wire format, field by field","How update_state derives the shapes","Why one sender per FFN rank is enough","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"CUDA graphs in AFD: FULL_DECODE_ONLY on both roles","u":"concepts/cuda-graphs.html","s":"AFD supports one CUDA graph mode, FULL_DECODE_ONLY. The attention side wraps native capture and replay to publish control; the FFN side keeps its own graph per token shape.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: One supported mode","Step 2: The attention side wraps native capture","Step 3: The attention side publishes control before each replay","Step 4: The FFN side keys one graph per shape","Step 5: The FFN side captures its own graph","Step 6: Custom ops keep the transfers capturable","Deep dive","The attention capture handshake, step by step","The FFN graph key and the graph cache","Why both sides must agree on shapes","Worked example: a 2A2F decode step with graphs on","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Dual-batch overlap (DBO): two stages per step","u":"concepts/dbo.html","s":"DBO splits one step into exactly two stages. While one stage waits on the FFN side, the other computes attention, so the two roles stay busy. AFD adds a yield op and scoped patches for DP1.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Exactly two stages","Step 2: Each stage carries a stage index on the wire","Step 3: The proxy yields between send and receive","Step 4: The thresholds decide when a step splits","Step 5: DP1 DBO through scoped patches","Deep dive","The two scoped patches, in detail","The thresholds, with defaults and numbers","The yield mechanism","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Deployment topologies: how to place the roles","u":"concepts/deployment-topologies.html","s":"The GPU recipes place the attention and FFN roles in five shapes: 2A2F and 4A4F colocation, 2P1A1F prefill/decode disaggregation with a proxy, a k8s multipod split, and the Qwen3.5 4A2F multipod. This page gives the process count, GPUs, ranks, flags, start order and ports of each.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: The rank rule (A = DP x TP, F = DP x TP)","Step 2: Colocation 2A2F (two servers, four GPUs)","Step 3: Colocation 4A4F (double both roles)","Step 4: Disaggregation 2P1A1F (prefillers and a proxy)","Step 5: Multipod on Kubernetes","Deep dive","Every GPU topology at a glance","Eager and graph variants","Start order and ports","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The FFN runtime: a connector-driven server loop","u":"concepts/ffn-runtime.html","s":"The FFN side has no real scheduler (a no-op stand-in) and no KV cache. A daemon thread reads one control payload per step and drives the FFN model runner, which runs the FFN or experts for each layer and stage.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Construction keeps only the FFN parts","Step 2: load_model loads the FFN model (and only its weights)","Step 3: The server loop runs one step per control payload","Step 4: _ffn_forward loops over layers and stages","Step 5: The FFN role never samples","Deep dive","Eager vs graph path","Worked example: one step on 2A2F with DBO","Shutdown closes the connector","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Model registration: the AFD<arch> wrappers","u":"concepts/model-registration.html","s":"AFD registers each supported architecture under an AFD<arch> name, as a lazy string. At build time it swaps the model to its AFD identity with a deepcopy. This page shows the registration, the identity swap, and which architectures map to which wrapper.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Register each AFD name","Step 2: The lazy \"module:Class\" form","Step 3: Detect an AFD model","Step 4: Swap the model identity","Step 5: Which architectures map to which wrapper","Deep dive","Why deepcopy, and the hf_text_config identity","README names a family; the code registers one string","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Model wrappers: the role-aware decoder layer","u":"concepts/model-wrappers.html","s":"Each supported model gets an AFD wrapper. The wrapper builds a decoder layer that constructs only the modules its role owns, and routes the FFN stage through the connector.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: One decoder-layer class, two roles","Step 2: The attention role builds native attention and one of four mlp branches","Step 3: The FFN role drops attention and keeps the real FFN","Step 4: RemoteFFNProxy sends, yields, then receives","Step 5: Weight loading keeps only this role's checkpoint paths","Deep dive","The other three model families, one at a time","The NPU wrappers (named here, covered on the overview page)","Worked example: which modules load for DeepSeek-V2-Lite","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The MoE handoff: where the gate runs","u":"concepts/moe-handoff.html","s":"The compute_gate_on_attention flag decides whether the MoE gate runs on the attention side or the FFN side. The choice changes which classes build, what crosses the connector, and which layers the FFN runs.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: The default: the gate runs on the FFN side","Step 2: Gate on the attention side: compute, then send the router logits","Step 3: The FFN side runs experts only, for expert layers only","Step 4: compute_experts_output needs the gate cleared","Deep dive","What crosses the wire, by case","The three attention-side MoE classes","The afd_balanced routing strategy (benchmarks only)","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Ascend NPU overview: the same contract, different transports","u":"concepts/npu-overview.html","s":"AFD on Ascend NPU keeps the same attention/FFN contract as GPU but uses two CAM connectors: CAMP2p (sync, with a control plane over HCCL and Gloo) and CAMAsync (async, no control plane, driven by its receive loop). This page maps the NPU workers, runners, model wrappers, patches and the operator loader, and points to the NPU docs.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: CAMP2p, the synchronous connector with a control plane","Step 2: CAMAsync has no control plane; its receive loop drives the FFN side","Step 3: The operator loader","Step 4: Async data-parallel patches, gated to NPU","Deep dive","NPU workers and runners","NPU model wrappers and Ascend patches","The NPU recipes and what they report","Where to read more: the NPU docs","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The plugin entry point: register_afd","u":"concepts/plugin-entry-point.html","s":"register_afd is the one function vLLM calls to install AFD. This page explains why it is safe to call many times, how it stays importable without vLLM, its version gate, and the order and error behaviour of its registration steps.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Two guards make it idempotent","Step 2: Import safety without vLLM","Step 3: The version gate warns, it does not stop","Step 4: What it registers, and in which order","Step 5: Which steps are wrapped in try/except","Deep dive","Idempotence across processes, and the order of the guards","Two different version checks","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Attention and FFN roles: what each server owns","u":"concepts/roles.html","s":"AFD runs the attention part and the FFN part as two server processes. This page shows what each role owns, and why the FFN side has no real scheduler, no KV cache and no sampler.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: Two workers, two runners","Step 2: The FFN EngineCore returns early","Step 3: The FFN worker has no KV cache and does not sample","Step 4: Each role builds half of a decoder layer","Step 5: Where requests go, and the start order","Deep dive","What each side owns, line by line","The FFN server loop","Start order and collective init","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Topology and role ranks: the AFD world","u":"concepts/topology.html","s":"The P2P connector builds one world, [F0..A0..], from the role ranks. This page shows how the role rank, the subgroups and the DP-metadata routes come from the topology sizes.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: The role rank","Step 2: The world order","Step 3: The subgroups","Step 4: The A ≥ F rule","Step 5: The DP-metadata routes","Step 6: The plugin-owned process groups","Deep dive","Worked example: 2A2F (DP1, TP2)","Worked example: 4A2F (the planes differ)","The process groups and their timeouts","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Tuning: the knobs the repo exposes","u":"concepts/tuning.html","s":"What afd-plugin lets you tune, and only what its code and READMEs state: the A:F rank ratio, DBO thresholds, CUDA graph capture, the group timeout, gate placement, the NPU CAM capacity, the profiler, the routing simulator and the benchmark tools. Every number is cited.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: The A:F rank ratio","Step 2: DBO thresholds","Step 3: CUDA graph capture size","Step 4: The group timeout","Step 5: Gate placement","Deep dive","BATCH_SIZE_FACTOR: the NPU CAM receive capacity","The profiler","The routing simulator","The benchmark tools and the result images","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Data on the wire: tensors per layer and stage","u":"concepts/wire-data.html","s":"The data plane moves hidden states between the two roles once per layer and stage. This page shows the tensors that cross, the transfer metadata, the fan-in and split, and the custom ops.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: What the attention rank sends","Step 2: The transfer metadata","Step 3: The FFN rank fans in its peers","Step 4: The FFN rank splits the output back","Step 5: The transfers are custom ops","Deep dive","Worked example: two peers into one FFN rank (4A2F)","The two optional tensors","Receive buffers and the current stream","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Worker selection: choosing the role worker","u":"concepts/worker-selection.html","s":"AFD chooses a role-specific worker class by rewriting parallel_config.worker_cls, but only when the user left it as \"auto\". This page shows the rewrite, the platform-to-worker table, the native resolution, the runner swap and the compatibility check.","sec":"AFD concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: The config patch records whether worker_cls was \"auto\"","Step 2: Rewrite only when it was \"auto\"","Step 3: The platform default maps to an AFD worker","Step 4: vLLM imports the chosen class","Step 5: The worker swaps the model runner in init_device","Deep dive","An explicit wrong worker is rejected, not fixed","Why the runner swap is a scoped module-attribute patch","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"AFD Plugin Explained","u":"index.html","s":"A study site for afd-plugin, the vLLM external plugin for Attention-FFN Disaggregation (AFD). It explains the code at two pinned commits.","sec":"Home","lvl":"","h":["At a glance","The system map","Attention server","FFN server","Around every step","Start here","How to use this site","What is not in this release","The code version this site explains"]},{"k":"page","t":"How vLLM plugins work","u":"learn/vllm-plugins.html","s":"vLLM reads entry points in the vllm.general_plugins group and calls each one at start-up. This page shows that mechanism, so the afd-plugin entry point makes sense.","sec":"Learn the basics","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: The five entry-point groups","Step 2: load_plugins_by_group and load_general_plugins","Step 3: When and in which processes","Step 4: How a plugin registers models, workers and config","Step 5: What a plugin can and cannot change","Deep dive","The 5 call sites, and the process each runs in","The guard, and why idempotence matters","In afd-plugin","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The vLLM v1 map","u":"learn/vllm-v1-map.html","s":"AFD hooks into a short chain of vLLM v1 processes and classes: the API server, the EngineCore, the executor, the worker, the Model Runner V2 and the model. This page names each box, with its vLLM line and the AFD hook on it.","sec":"Learn the basics","lvl":"Beginner","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Deep dive","How many processes, and where each hook runs","The FFN EngineCore is the one box that breaks the chain","In afd-plugin","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"What is AFD?","u":"learn/what-is-afd.html","s":"AFD runs the attention part and the FFN/MoE part of one model in separate server processes. This page explains why that helps, what xAyF means, and how AFD differs from prefill/decode disaggregation and from plain expert parallelism.","sec":"Learn the basics","lvl":"Beginner","h":["The idea","The picture","Deep dive","AFD vs. prefill/decode disaggregation","AFD vs. expert parallelism (EP)","The xAyF topology name and the rank-count rule","In afd-plugin","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"AFD config reference: the additional_config[\"afd\"] keys","u":"reference/afd-config.html","s":"Every key of --additional-config \"afd\", with its alias, type, default and validation, plus the connector_extra_config keys per connector and a worked example from a real recipe.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["How the config is passed","The keys","The afd keys","connector_extra_config by connector","Validation rules","Where each value is read, and what it changes","Where the final value is set","Worked example: the recipe's afd block","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Reference: compat patches","u":"reference/compat-patches.html","s":"Every AFD compat patch, its vLLM target at the pinned commit, how and when it installs, what it changes, and the page that explains it. Hand-checked, one row per replaced function.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"Advanced","h":["How to read this table","GPU patches","config_validation (config_validation.py)","engine_core (engine_core.py)","gpu_mrv2_ubatching (gpu_mrv2_ubatching.py)","NPU-gated patches","async_dp_engine (async_dp_engine.py)","async_dp_forward_context (async_dp_forward_context.py)","NPU device patches","What is not patched","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Environment variables reference","u":"reference/env-vars.html","s":"Every environment variable that afd-plugin reads or sets, generated from the source, with defaults and read sites. A hand section lists the vLLM variables that AFD depends on.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["How the plugin uses the environment","Every variable the plugin reads or sets","What each group does","Multiprocessing","Build-time (setup.py)","Profilers","Benchmark and routing","Ascend (NPU) runtime","vLLM variables that AFD depends on","Refreshing this page","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"FAQ","u":"reference/faq.html","s":"Short answers with citations. Where docs and code disagree, known open questions, and common practical questions about running and understanding AFD.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["Where do docs and code disagree?","Known open questions","Practical questions","Does AFD change the vLLM source?","Why does the FFN server reject a request?","Why does the FFN server have no scheduler?","Can the attention ranks be fewer than the FFN ranks (A < F)?","How do the rank counts relate to DP and TP?","Which CUDA graph modes are allowed?","Where does sampling run?","Why are there two vLLM version checks?","What happens on a different vLLM version?","Which models does AFD support?","Which connector is the default, and on which device?","How do I benchmark a recipe?","Why two servers for one model?","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Recipes reference","u":"reference/recipes.html","s":"Every recipe script under recipe/, generated from the source, with hardware, connector, model, topology, role, DP/TP, mode, DBO thresholds and ports. Hand notes cover the parts the parser cannot read.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["How to read this table","What the parser cannot read","DBO thresholds in the recipes","Running a recipe","Refreshing this page","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Tour: Life of a request","u":"tours/life-of-a-request.html","s":"Follow one request from the HTTP call to the sampled token, across the attention server and the FFN server, stop by stop. The control plane runs once per step; the data plane runs once per layer and stage.","sec":"Guided tours","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","1The client calls the attention API server","2EngineCore and the scheduler batch the request","3The executor calls the AFD attention worker","4The AFD runner wraps native execute_model","5The execution context installs the metadata provider","6The control plane publishes DP metadata (once per step)","Why 'once per step', and the two code paths","7The native model forward runs the decoder layers","8The attention side sends hidden states (per layer and stage)","The shapes on the wire","9The FFN server loop receives the step","10The FFN runner computes each layer and stage","11The attention side receives the FFN output and continues the layer","12The attention side samples the token and returns it","The loop","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Tour: One decode step","u":"tours/one-decode-step.html","s":"Follow one decode step on 2A2F with DBO and FULL_DECODE_ONLY graphs: one control-plane publish, graph replay on both sides, two stages of send and receive per layer, then one native sampling call.","sec":"Guided tours","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","1The scheduler builds one step","2The runner wraps native execute_model","3The AFD context installs the provider and the replay hook","4The control plane publishes DP metadata once","The two publish paths, both once per step","5The attention side replays its graph","6Per layer and stage: send, yield, the other stage","7The FFN side receives one DP-metadata payload per step","8The FFN side looks up and replays its graph","9Per layer and stage: recv, compute, send","10After the last layer: sample, then output","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Tour: Run a recipe","u":"tours/run-a-recipe.html","s":"Read the 2a2f_graph_dbo_dp1tp2 recipe line by line. Each flag, the afd block, the FFN server, the start order, and how to send and benchmark a request, all cited to the script.","sec":"Guided tours","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","1Turn the V2 runner on","2The attention serve command and the parallel sizes","3The afd block in --additional-config","4The batch, DBO and CUDA graph flags","5The HTTP port and backgrounding","6The FFN server: the same block, role ffn","7Start order: background both, then wait","Run it from the repository root","8Send a request, then benchmark it","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Tour: Start-up","u":"tours/start-up.html","s":"Follow both servers from vllm serve to ready, stop by stop: plugin load, the worker_cls rewrite, the FFN EngineCore early return, the runner swap, the connector handshake, and graph capture on both roles.","sec":"Guided tours","lvl":"Advanced","h":["The idea","The picture","1vllm serve starts both roles","2vLLM loads the plugin in process 0","3register_afd installs the patches","4The config patch rewrites worker_cls","5The FFN EngineCore returns early","6The workers load the plugin and swap the runner","7load_model defers the connector init (attention)","8The two roles meet at the connector handshake","The three process groups and their timeouts","9KV cache on the attention side only","10Both sides capture CUDA graphs","11The FFN server loop starts","12Ready","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Glossary","u":"reference/glossary.html","s":"Short definitions of every technical term on this site, with links to the pages that explain them.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"","h":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H","L","M","P","R","S","U","V","W","X"]},{"k":"term","t":"ACL graph","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-acl-graph","s":"The Ascend NPU form of a captured graph, the NPU counterpart of a CUDA graph. The NPU validator accepts the FULL and FULL_DECODE_ONLY modes.","a":["ACL graph mode","Ascend graph"]},{"k":"term","t":"additional_config","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-additional-config","s":"A vLLM config field. The plugin reads its \"afd\" key for the role, the connector, the topology sizes and the host/port.","a":["--additional-config"]},{"k":"term","t":"AFD","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-afd","s":"Attention-FFN Disaggregation. The attention part and the feed-forward (FFN/MoE) part of a model run in separate server processes and exchange hidden states.","a":["Attention-FFN Disaggregation","attention-ffn disaggregation"]},{"k":"term","t":"afd_balanced routing","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-afd-balanced","s":"A deterministic, EP-balanced MoE routing strategy for benchmarks. It returns uniform weights and fixed expert ids, so it changes model outputs.","a":["AFDBalancedRoutingStrategy","routing simulator"]},{"k":"term","t":"AFDConfig","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-afd-config","s":"The plugin's own configuration object. It holds the role, the connector name, the topology sizes and the rendezvous host/port.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"AFD forward metadata","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-afd-forward-metadata","s":"A sidecar object that AFD attaches to the vLLM forward context. It holds the connector, the stage index and count, the transaction id, and the token offsets and lengths.","a":["AFDForwardContextMetadata","afd_metadata","forward-context sidecar"]},{"k":"term","t":"AFD world","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-afd-world","s":"The connector's process group across both roles. The P2P connector orders it [F0..A0..]: FFN ranks first, then attention ranks.","a":["AFD NCCL world","afd world","AFD world group"]},{"k":"term","t":"async_dp","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-async-dp","s":"An AFD config flag. It is NPU-only: it turns on the async data-parallel patches, is gated at run time, and must be false on GPU.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"attention role","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-attention-role","s":"The side that runs attention and sends hidden states to the FFN side. The default role of a process.","a":["attention side"]},{"k":"term","t":"attention server","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-attention-server","s":"The vllm serve process that plays the attention role. It owns the API server, the scheduler and the KV cache.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"BATCH_SIZE_FACTOR","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-batch-size-factor","s":"An Ascend NPU environment variable that multiplies the CAM receive capacity (base 262144 tokens), with native float rounding. Default 1.","a":["CAM receive capacity"]},{"k":"term","t":"CAM","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-cam","s":"The Ascend NPU communication operators used by the NPU connectors. One connector is synchronous, the other asynchronous.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"citation","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-citation","s":"A link to a file and line in the code. Every claim about the code on this site has one.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"commit","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-commit","s":"One saved version of a git repository. A 40-character id names it. This site explains one pinned commit of each repository.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"compute_gate_on_attention","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-compute-gate-on-attention","s":"An AFD config flag. When true, the attention side computes the MoE gate and sends the router logits to the FFN side.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"connector","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-connector","s":"The object that moves hidden states between the attention role and the FFN role. Each connector picks one transport.","a":["AFD connector"]},{"k":"term","t":"connector_extra_config","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-connector-extra-config","s":"A mapping of connector-specific options inside the AFD config. Each connector declares which keys it accepts, and rejects the rest.","a":["connector extra config"]},{"k":"term","t":"control plane","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-control-plane","s":"The path that carries DP metadata (per-stage token counts) from the attention side to the FFN side. It is optional.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"custom op","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-custom-op","s":"A function registered as torch.ops.vllm.* so torch.compile and CUDA graphs can trace it. The P2P sends and receives are custom ops.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"data plane","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-data-plane","s":"The path that carries the hidden-state tensors between the two roles. It is the connector's send and receive calls.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"DBO","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-dbo","s":"Dual-batch overlap. The step is split into two microbatches so compute and communication overlap. AFD uses exactly two.","a":["dual-batch overlap"]},{"k":"term","t":"DBO yield","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-dbo-yield","s":"A custom op that hands control to the other ubatch thread. The AFD proxy calls it between the send and the receive, so the second stage runs while the first waits.","a":["manual_dbo_yield","dbo yield"]},{"k":"term","t":"DP metadata","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-dp-metadata","s":"The per-step data-parallel metadata the attention side sends to the FFN side on the control plane. It carries the per-stage token counts that set the tensor shapes.","a":["data-parallel metadata"]},{"k":"term","t":"EngineCore","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-enginecore","s":"The vLLM v1 engine core process. On the FFN role it returns early after the executor is built: no scheduler, no loop.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"entry point","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-entry-point","s":"A Python packaging hook. vLLM reads the vllm.general_plugins entry points and calls each one at start-up.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"FFN graph key","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-ffn-graph-key","s":"A hashable key built from the per-stage DP metadata. The FFN runner uses it to store and look up one captured CUDA graph per token shape.","a":["make_ffn_graph_key","graph key"]},{"k":"term","t":"FFN role","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-ffn-role","s":"The side that runs the feed-forward and MoE experts. It receives hidden states, computes, and sends the result back.","a":["FFN side"]},{"k":"term","t":"FFN server","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-ffn-server","s":"The vllm serve process that plays the FFN role. It has no API server, no scheduler and no KV cache; a connector loop drives it.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"FFN server loop","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-ffn-server-loop","s":"The connector-driven daemon thread on the FFN worker. It waits for DP metadata, then runs the FFN compute for one step. No scheduler drives it.","a":["FFN loop","server loop"]},{"k":"term","t":"FULL_DECODE_ONLY","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-full-decode-only","s":"The only CUDA graph mode that AFD supports on GPU. Graphs capture decode steps only; other batches run eager.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"general plugin","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-general-plugin","s":"A vLLM plugin in the vllm.general_plugins group. vLLM loads it with load_general_plugins in several processes.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"HCCL","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-hccl","s":"Huawei Collective Communication Library, the Ascend NPU collective transport. The CAMP2p connector carries hidden states over HCCL and DP metadata over a smaller Gloo group.","a":["Huawei Collective Communication Library"]},{"k":"term","t":"load_general_plugins","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-load-general-plugins","s":"The vLLM function that loads every plugin in the vllm.general_plugins group, then calls each one. It runs once per process.","a":["load_general_plugins()"]},{"k":"term","t":"ModelRegistry","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-model-registry","s":"vLLM's registry of model architectures. A plugin adds a model with register_model, using a lazy \"module:Class\" string.","a":["ModelRegistry.register_model"]},{"k":"term","t":"Model Runner V2","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-model-runner-v2","s":"The vLLM v1 model runner, version 2. Both AFD roles need it on; the README sets VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=1. AFD adds a seam over it.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"model wrapper","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-model-wrapper","s":"An AFD<arch> model class that makes a model role-aware. On the attention side the FFN stage becomes a remote proxy.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"multipod","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-multipod","s":"A Kubernetes deployment that puts one AFD role per pod. Each vllm serve is gated on a POD environment variable, so with POD unset nothing runs.","a":["multi-pod","multipod deployment"]},{"k":"term","t":"P2P subgroup","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-p2p-subgroup","s":"In the GPU P2P connector, one FFN rank plus the attention ranks mapped to it. The FFN rank is subgroup rank 0.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"patch","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-patch","s":"A compat module that replaces a vLLM function at load time. The plugin applies a small set of patches.","a":["compat patch"]},{"k":"term","t":"prefill/decode disaggregation","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-pd-disaggregation","s":"A serving layout that runs prefill and decode in separate processes. Each process still runs the whole model. It splits by phase, not by model part.","a":["PD disaggregation","prefill-decode disaggregation"]},{"k":"term","t":"afd-plugin","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-plugin","s":"The vLLM external plugin that adds AFD. vLLM loads it; it changes no vLLM source file.","a":["the plugin","afd plugin"]},{"k":"term","t":"PyNccl","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-pynccl","s":"vLLM's thin NCCL wrapper, PyNcclCommunicator. The GPU P2P connector uses it to send and receive tensors.","a":["PyNcclCommunicator"]},{"k":"term","t":"register_afd()","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-register-afd","s":"The plugin entry function. It applies the compat patches and registers the routing strategy, the DBO op and the model wrappers.","a":["register_afd"]},{"k":"term","t":"remote experts","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-remote-experts","s":"A parameter-free MoE shell on the attention role. It keeps the native MoE forward but sends the expert compute to the FFN role.","a":["remote experts shell","AFDDeepseekV2RemoteExpertsMoE"]},{"k":"term","t":"RemoteFFNProxy","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-remote-ffn-proxy","s":"A parameter-free module on the attention side. It replaces the FFN stage of a decoder layer and uses the connector instead.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"rendezvous","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-rendezvous","s":"The host and port where the two roles meet to set up the connector process groups. Both servers use the same host and port.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"role rank","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-role-rank","s":"The rank of a process inside its own role group. It comes from the DP, PCP and TP placement.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"scoped patch","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-scoped-patch","s":"A patch that is active only inside a context manager, for the time of one AFD call. The code restores the original function on exit.","a":["scoped monkey-patch","context-manager patch"]},{"k":"term","t":"stage","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-stage","s":"The DBO ubatch index of one transfer on the wire. A connector sends one transfer per layer and stage.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"step","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-step","s":"One engine iteration on the attention server: the scheduler picks a batch, the model runs all layers once, and vLLM samples. The FFN server follows each step.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"ubatch","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-ubatch","s":"A microbatch: one of the two token groups that DBO runs at the same time. On the wire its index is the stage.","a":["microbatch"]},{"k":"term","t":"version gate","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-version-gate","s":"A check that decides whether a patch installs. is_target_vllm_compatible allows the target vLLM release and any dev build; on a mismatch, no patch installs.","a":["is_target_vllm_compatible","install gate"]},{"k":"term","t":"worker_cls","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-worker-cls","s":"The vLLM parallel-config field that names the worker class. The plugin rewrites it, but only when it is \"auto\".","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"xAyF","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-xayf","s":"A topology name: x attention ranks and y FFN ranks, for example 2A2F. Use it only in topology names, not in prose.","a":["2A2F","4A4F"]}];
